.TH NCLR-BACKEND 7 "August 2026" "nclr 0.1.0" "File Formats and Protocols"
.SH NAME
nclr-backend \- nclr backend execution protocol and contract
.SH DESCRIPTION
Backends are short-lived executables located under
.B /usr/libexec/nclr/
(nclr-lba, nclr-sim, nclr-scsi, nclr-sd-native, nclr-controller). The core
executes exactly one request per invocation; backends must not daemonize,
keep state, or open any device path themselves.
.SH INVOCATION
.TP
.B nclr-<id> probe --request-fd 4 --device-fd 3 [--events-fd 5]
.TP
.B nclr-<id> plan  --request-fd 4 --device-fd 3 [--events-fd 5]
.TP
.B nclr-<id> run   --request-fd 4 --device-fd 3 [--events-fd 5]
.TP
.B nclr-<id> status --request-fd 4 --device-fd 3 [--events-fd 5]
.TP
.B nclr-<id> recover --request-fd 4 --device-fd 3 [--events-fd 5]
.PP
The device fd is opened and validated by the core (fstat, sysfs
correspondence); the request is read from the request fd until EOF. Optional
additional device fds (sg, usbfs) are inherited at fd 6 and above and are
described in the request's extra_fds array. Physical salvage outputs are a
paired physical-image and physical-map fd; paths are never sent to a backend.
Backends write one final JSON
response to stdout and exit; diagnostics go to stderr; NDJSON progress events
go to the events fd.
.SH REQUEST
All fields optional unless noted:
.TP
.B api
Protocol version (1).
.TP
.B op
probe | plan | run | status | recover.
.TP
.B action
For run: the action id (inventory, lba-prbs-write, device-user-area-erase,
enumerate-blocks, erase-data-blocks, qualify-blocks,
verify-physical-erasure, salvage-physical, rebuild-bbt-ftl,
scratch-test, ...).
.TP
.B seed
PRBS seed (plan hash) for pattern actions.
.TP
.B params
Action parameters (e.g. scratch-test {start, count}).
.TP
.B extra_fds
[{fd, role}] inherited extra device fds.
.SH RESPONSE
.TP
.B ok
boolean.
.TP
.B backend / version / match / grade_ceiling / capabilities
probe identity data. Capabilities use the protocol vocabulary
(READ_IDENTITY, ERASE_USER_AREA, CONTROLLER_REINITIALIZE, PHYSICAL_SCOPE,
PHYSICAL_SALVAGE,
SD_VENDOR_HEALTH, LBA_PRBS_WRITE, ...).
.TP
.B action_results
Array of {status: ok|partial|error|interrupted, errors, ...} for run
actions. Action failures are reported in the response with a clean exit; a
nonzero exit indicates a protocol/transport failure. A status of
.B interrupted
(reported with a clean exit) means the device operation was stopped in a
resumable state (for example a busy timeout mid-erase); the core exits 75
and the run can be continued with
.B nclr resume
instead of treating the operation as failed.
.SH CONTRACT RULES
.IP 1.
The backend operates only on the inherited fds; it must never open a device
path (SEC-001).
.IP 2.
One request per process; no RPC server, no sockets, no database.
.IP 3.
Destructive operations require an exact production-profile match and the
declared trust state.
.IP 4.
IMMED long-running operations (e.g. SANITIZE) return {started: true}; the
core polls the status op and the backend reports progress until completion.
.IP 5.
Per-block records (erase results) are returned in action_results; the core
streams them to the evidence directory.
.IP 6.
Capabilities are declared only when the profile exactly matches and the
preconditions hold; existence of a function is not a capability.
.SH MANIFESTS
A backend manifest <dir>/<id>.toml declares id, trust, profile, version and
an optional sha256 digest of the binary. The core reads those fields,
verifies the digest when present and records backend version/digest in
reports; manifests with trust = "research" are refused for destructive
operations. (The id and api are implied by the filename and protocol; a
schema field, when present, is informational.)
.SH SEE ALSO
nclr(1), nclr-lab(1).
